1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:05,724
The brush uncovers iron — a tall, slender shaft, and the surface is almost clean.

2
00:00:05,724 --> 00:00:10,672
Iron left standing in the open for sixteen centuries should be a heap of rust.

3
00:00:12,400 --> 00:00:18,282
This one is not, and the reason is chemistry: the composition of the metal forms a protective layer.

4
00:00:18,282 --> 00:00:20,844
Whoever made it did not know the chemistry.

5
00:00:20,844 --> 00:00:22,367
They knew the craft.

6
00:00:25,800 --> 00:00:31,211
Texts, copied and recopied: mathematical and astronomical treatises, and plays and poems.

7
00:00:31,211 --> 00:00:38,065
And inscriptions, which among other things tell us that the numerals and the dating systems were in everyday use,

8
00:00:38,065 --> 00:00:40,109
not just in scholars' books.

9
00:00:43,133 --> 00:00:48,472
Start with Aryabhata, born in 476 CE, who wrote the Aryabhatiya at the age of twenty-three.

10
00:00:48,472 --> 00:00:53,986
In it he gives a value for pi accurate to four decimal places and describes it as approximate,

11
00:00:53,986 --> 00:00:57,013
which is a remarkable thing to be clear about.

12
00:01:00,967 --> 00:01:08,937
He explains eclipses correctly, as shadows — the earth's shadow on the moon, the moon's shadow on the earth — rather than by myth.

13
00:01:08,937 --> 00:01:15,504
And he states that the apparent daily motion of the stars is caused by the earth rotating on its axis.

14
00:01:17,333 --> 00:01:21,546
And the thing that changed the world: the decimal place-value system with a zero.

15
00:01:21,546 --> 00:01:25,914
The idea that the same nine digits mean different amounts depending on their column,

16
00:01:25,914 --> 00:01:32,071
and that an empty column needs a symbol of its own, is one of the most consequential inventions anybody has made.

17
00:01:35,967 --> 00:01:39,523
It developed in India over this period, passed to the Arab world,

18
00:01:39,523 --> 00:01:44,943
and reached Europe centuries later — which is why Europeans called the digits Arabic numerals.

19
00:01:46,900 --> 00:01:47,892
Others to know.

20
00:01:47,892 --> 00:01:53,470
Varahamihira, whose Brihat Samhita is a compendium of astronomy, astrology and much else,

21
00:01:53,470 --> 00:01:57,985
and who compiled earlier astronomical schools including Greek ones.

22
00:02:00,400 --> 00:02:06,162
Brahmagupta, slightly later, who set out rules for working with zero and with negative numbers.

23
00:02:06,162 --> 00:02:14,324
And in medicine, the Charaka and Sushruta traditions — older than this period but compiled and commented on in it — covering diagnosis,

24
00:02:14,324 --> 00:02:16,004
treatment and surgery.

25
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:21,605
The literature is Sanskrit and the best-known name is Kalidasa,

26
00:02:21,605 --> 00:02:26,861
author of the play Abhijnanashakuntalam, the poems Meghaduta and Raghuvamsha, and more.

27
00:02:29,267 --> 00:02:31,853
Also of this broad period: the Panchatantra,

28
00:02:31,853 --> 00:02:38,060
animal fables that travelled further than almost any Indian book; and the final compilation of the Puranas.

29
00:02:38,060 --> 00:02:45,888
Sanskrit becomes the language of courts and of learning across the subcontinent, which is itself a historical fact worth noticing.

30
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:49,996
So: golden age?

31
00:02:49,996 --> 00:02:56,520
The case for is real — the achievements of this period are extraordinary and they are concentrated.

32
00:02:58,733 --> 00:03:04,323
The case against is that the phrase describes the experience of a small, courtly, educated minority;

33
00:03:04,323 --> 00:03:09,414
that the same period's legal texts narrow the position of women and of lower-ranked groups;

34
00:03:09,414 --> 00:03:13,786
and that the sources which survive are precisely the ones that elite produced.

35
00:03:13,786 --> 00:03:19,455
A good answer gives the achievements their full weight and then says who the age was golden for.

36
00:03:24,300 --> 00:03:27,014
Here is the memory tip, carved in stone.

37
00:03:27,014 --> 00:03:31,616
Zero, pi, and the spinning earth — three things worked out here.

38
00:03:36,400 --> 00:03:37,193
Dig Quiz.

39
00:03:37,193 --> 00:03:38,701
Pause and name it.

40
00:03:38,701 --> 00:03:46,239
First: the mathematician and astronomer born in 476 CE who said the earth rotates on its axis.

41
00:03:46,239 --> 00:03:47,411
Aryabhata.

42
00:03:50,100 --> 00:03:57,444
Second: the numeral system, with a symbol for an empty column, that passed from India to the Arab world and then to Europe.

43
00:03:59,400 --> 00:04:02,583
The decimal place-value system, with zero.

44
00:04:02,583 --> 00:04:06,803
Third: the author of Abhijnanashakuntalam and Meghaduta.

45
00:04:06,803 --> 00:04:07,843
Kalidasa.

46
00:04:15,267 --> 00:04:20,864
In Mains, a frequent question asks whether the Gupta period deserves to be called a golden age.

47
00:04:20,864 --> 00:04:25,829
Give the achievements, then ask the question the label avoids: golden for whom?

48
00:04:27,400 --> 00:04:31,105
The iron pillar takes its place in the gallery, still barely rusted.

49
00:04:45,400 --> 00:04:48,867
Next gallery: the north is pulled together one more time,

50
00:04:48,867 --> 00:04:53,828
by a king we know unusually well because two very different writers described him.

51
00:04:53,828 --> 00:04:57,535
To catch it when it's out, subscribe to Gyaan-X Studios.

